# Copy this file to ".env". Everything is optional: winctl works with no
# configuration at all. These variables only concern two things -- the
# send_summary tool, and read-only mode.
#
# WHERE does winctl look for this file? It does not guess. In order:
#   1. the path passed by the calling program to load_env();
#   2. the WINCTL_ENV_FILE environment variable;
#   3. the .env file in the current working directory.
#
# An MCP server is started by its client, with the working directory of the
# session: option 3 therefore only happens to be right by chance. For a durable
# installation, name the file when registering the server:
#
#   claude mcp add winctl --scope user \
#     --env WINCTL_ENV_FILE=C:\path\to\.env -- winctl-mcp
#
# The notification_status tool reports the file actually loaded.

# --- Telegram (recommended: the quickest to set up) -------------------------
# 1. On Telegram, write to @BotFather -> /newbot -> follow the instructions.
# 2. Paste the token below.
# 3. Send any message to your new bot.
# 4. Ask the agent to call the telegram_find_chat_id tool to get the ID.
TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=
TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID=

# --- Email (alternative) ----------------------------------------------------
# send_summary falls back to email if Telegram is not configured. SMTP_HOST and
# SMTP_TO are the minimum. For Gmail, use an "app password", not the account
# password: https://myaccount.google.com/apppasswords
SMTP_HOST=
SMTP_PORT=587
SMTP_USER=
SMTP_PASSWORD=
SMTP_FROM=
SMTP_TO=

# --- Safety -----------------------------------------------------------------
# Set to 1 for an "observation" mode: screenshots, window listing and clipboard
# reads allowed; keyboard, mouse, window moves, application launches and
# PowerShell blocked with an explicit message.
WINCTL_READONLY=
